{
  "ID": "2.1",
  "Context/Background": "ASXL1 plays key roles in epigenetic regulation of early developmental gene expression. De novo protein-truncating mutations in ASXL1 cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), a rare neurodevelopmental condition characterized by severe intellectual disabilities, distinctive facial features, hypertrichosis, increased risk of Wilms tumor, and variable congenital anomalies, including heart defects and severe skeletal defects giving rise to a typical BOS posture. These BOS-causing ASXL1 variants are also high-prevalence somatic driver mutations in acute myeloid leukemia.  We used primary cells from individuals with BOS (n = 18) and controls (n = 49) to dissect gene regulatory changes caused by ASXL1 mutations using comprehensive multiomics assays for chromatin accessibility (ATAC-seq), DNA methylation, histone methylation binding, and transcriptome in peripheral blood and skin fibroblasts.\n",
  "Prompt": "\nBased on the results of the blood and fibroblast transcriptomic DEG analyses of BOS vs controls, provide a discussion of the common and distinct pathways found between blood and fibroblast and any relevance to the presence in the BOS samples of ASXL1 truncating mutations.",
  "mode": "Baseline",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 23:30:33",
  "elapsed_seconds": 155.37,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nASXL1 truncating mutations cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), a neurodevelopmental disorder with systemic anomalies. Transcriptomic analyses of blood (n=49 controls vs. BOS) and fibroblasts (n=18 controls vs. BOS) revealed distinct and shared differentially expressed genes (DEGs). Blood DEGs (1166 up, 952 down) and fibroblast DEGs (129 up, 48 down) highlight tissue-specific dysregulation. Common DEGs (25 genes, e.g., *VANGL2*, *GRIK5*) suggest conserved pathways, while tissue-specific DEGs (e.g., *SLC7A3* in blood, *UGT3A2* in fibroblasts) implicate context-dependent effects of ASXL1 loss.  \n\n**Keywords**  \nASXL1 truncating mutations, Bohring-Opitz syndrome, transcriptomic dysregulation, blood, fibroblasts, developmental pathways, chromatin regulation.  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \n*VANGL2*, *GRIK5*, *GREM2*, *CNTN4*, *SLC7A3*, *UGT3A2*, *STMN2*.  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \n*Fibroblast-Up*:  \n- **KEGG_2016 (UP)**: Axon guidance (e.g., *CNTN4*, *GNAO1*, *VANGL2*), cell adhesion molecules (e.g., *CNTN6*).  \n- **GO_Biological_Process_2023 (UP)**: Neuron development (e.g., *GRIK5*, *VANGL2*), extracellular matrix organization (e.g., *FRRS1L*).  \n\n*Blood-Up*:  \n- **KEGG_2016 (UP)**: Neuroactive ligand-receptor interaction (e.g., *SLC7A3*, *GRIK5*), hematopoietic cell lineage (e.g., *DNTT*).  \n- **GO_Biological_Process_2023 (UP)**: Immune response (e.g., *CBX2*, *SHISA2*), skeletal development (e.g., *MYT1L*).  \n\n*Shared-Up*:  \n- **Wnt signaling (VANGL2)** and **neurodevelopmental pathways (GRIK5)**.  \n\n*Fibroblast-Down*:  \n- **GO_Biological_Process_2023 (DOWN)**: Cell cycle regulation (e.g., *STMN2*), extracellular matrix (e.g., *GREM2*).  \n\n*Blood-Down*:  \n- **KEGG_2016 (DOWN)**: Complement cascade (e.g., *CFH*), developmental patterning (e.g., *GREM2*).  \n\n**Targeted Subset**  \n- *ASXL1*-related pathways: *VANGL2* (Wnt signaling) and *GREM2* (developmental patterning) are downregulated in both tissues, aligning with BOS skeletal anomalies. *SLC7A3* (blood-specific upregulation) and *UGT3A2* (fibroblast-specific upregulation) suggest tissue-specific compensatory mechanisms.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)**  \n| Gene     | Direction | Role/Function                  | Pathway(s)                |  \n|----------|-----------|--------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| *VANGL2* | Up        | Planar cell polarity (Wnt)     | Axon guidance, development|  \n| *GRIK5*  | Up        | Glutamate receptor (neuronal)  | Neurodevelopment          |  \n| *GREM2*  | Down      | BMP antagonist (development)   | Skeletal patterning       |  \n| *STMN2*  | Down      | Microtubule destabilization    | Cell cycle                |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \n- Shared pathways (e.g., Wnt signaling) suggest systemic ASXL1 effects.  \n- Blood-specific DEGs (e.g., *SLC7A3*, *CBX2*) reflect immune/hematopoietic dysregulation, while fibroblast-specific DEGs (e.g., *CNTN4*, *FRRS1L*) highlight extracellular matrix/cell adhesion defects.  \n\n**Implications**  \nASXL1 truncations disrupt developmental and cell adhesion pathways in a tissue-specific manner, contributing to BOS multi-system anomalies. Upregulated *VANGL2* and downregulated *GREM2* may drive skeletal defects, while blood-specific DEGs could link to cancer susceptibility.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. Validate *VANGL2* and *GREM2* expression in BOS patient tissues to confirm pathway dysregulation.  \n2. Investigate ASXL1 chromatin binding at DEG loci (e.g., *CNTN4*, *SLC7A3*) using ChIP-seq to establish direct regulatory effects.  \n3. Assess Wnt/BMP signaling activity in BOS models to mechanistically link ASXL1 mutations to developmental defects.  \n\n**References**  \nNot applicable (no external PMIDs validated).",
